WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.100
Hi, guys today I'm going to talk about 
whether you should learn

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:09.320
a language that is a marketable skill 
or a language that is "useless".

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.540
Good evening, guys, 
how are you doing today?

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.160
So you are interested in learning languages,
you have looked into it.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.720
You think it's a interesting thing to do.
You want to be bilingual

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.480
But you are wondering which language to learn.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.620
Maybe you should study Spanish
because there are lots of Spanish-speakers.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:37.240
Maybe you should study Chinese, because Chinese will
be the language of the 21st century, some people say.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:43.160
Maybe you should study Arabic, because there are 
lots of Arabic speakers in lots of countries .

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.060
Maybe you should choose one of those.
But maybe you're interested in Burmese.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.680
Maybe, for some reason yours,
you want to learn Burmese.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.880
Or maybe you're interested in Hebrew,
which is only spoken in Israel.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.200
Or maybe you're interested in Esperanto 
which is spoken nowhere.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.860
Maybe you have some kind of desire or some interest 
to learn some language that's not really spoken 
that widely spoken in a lot of places.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.740
So a lot of people ask me which kind 
of language they should study.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.320
The first foreign language 
that I ever studied was Hebrew.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.520
And that's a language that's only spoken
in one place. Only in Israel. Pretty much.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:28.120
So it's not a language that was really 
all that useful for me in a lot of places.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.580
Unless I met some Israeli people here and there 
and spoke Hebrew with them,

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:38.020
basically it was just I learned it out 
of irrational cultural interest.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:42.340
I became interested in that country and 
in that language and I really wanted to study it.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:49.440
And people at the time said: "why don't you study French?
Why don't you study Spanish? 
Why don't you study Chinese?"

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.880
Uh... I kind of tried to do that.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.600
But I was always drawn back to Hebrew,
because that's where my interest was.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.400
So I couldn't really focus on the other languages.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:04.540
So I think that's really the answer to this dilemma 
of which language, which kind of language to study?

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.280
It's study the one that you are passionate about,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.200
the one that you can focus on, 
the one that engages you.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.520
Because learning a language is, well, 
if we're talking about learning a language, full-on,

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.460
like all-in, to learn it well, then
that's a process that takes quite a while.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:26.900
You have to work at it over a couple years, a few years 
if we're talking about getting really good at it, right?

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:32.240
Including being literate, you can be conversant
in a few months but if you want to ...

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:37.820
if you want to be really good at it, all language skills, 
that it takes longer than that.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:44.060
So if you're all-in, if you're long term in a language, 
then  you have to be really interested in it.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.320
You have to be motivated and that motivation comes
from just your deep interest, that irrational...

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:56.720
irrational magnetism of that language that you feel, right?

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.720
If you decide to learn a language 
because it's a marketable skill.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:08.300
That's what some people think they should do, 
they want to learn a language because, someday, 
in the future, it might be... marketable,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.560
it might be useful on my resume, 
it might help me get a job.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.560
Um... Well.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.300
It probably won't because, first of all, you won't 
have the motivation to stick with it. Most likely.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.740
When you learn a language, there are a lot of 
speech bumps along the way, right?

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.160
It's never just a smooth road. 
There are always times when your skill drops.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.860
There are times, when there's a plateau for a long time.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:36.160
plateau you get frustrated sometimes
because your level isn't improving.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:42.260
Now, if you really love the language, then you just fight
through it and stick it out, because that's a welcome challenge

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.020
But... if you're not really interested in the language 
in the first place, if you're just learning it,

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:52.420
because, someday, it might be useful on my resume, 
then you're going to say "Screw it!" and give up probably.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.600
So the best thing is is to stick 
with the language that you're interested in.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:05.120
If you happen to be passionate about a major global 
language like Spanish or Chinese or maybe Arabic or...

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:12.780
one of these, then consider yourself lucky
 because you will have the motivation 
that comes from your interest in the language.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:19.480
But, at the end of the road, you will have that skill that 
can be applied to potentially to a lot of different jobs or...

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:25.440
travel in a lot of different places. So...
you lucked out. Your interest landed in the right place.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.900
Unfortunately you can't control where
your interest lands. But you were lucky.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.520
So... go with that if that's the case.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:39.440
If you happen to be interested in less commonly spoken 
niche language, Hebrew or Burmese or whatever like I said,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.440
something that's only spoken in one country,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.580
Go with that anyway.
There are a lot of benefits you can get.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:52.540
One of the benefits is that you learn how to learn 
languages. You learn the process of learning a language.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:58.240
Maybe now I'm learning Hebrew. That was 
the case for me, my first foreign language.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.840
I was learning Hebrew,
but I learned how to study, right?

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.600
I learned what you need to put in.
I learned what about the consistency.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.880
I learned about how to study vocabulary,
I learned about ...

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:13.120
the importance of training my ears
and connecting my brain and my mouth,

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.980
for oral communication. I learned 
all these things that I applied to later languages.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:24.260
So now, even though I don't really use Hebrew that often, except to listen to Hebrew media on the internet,

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.780
It's still helped me because
it helps me learn other languages now.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:37.400
So that was my base. It was where I gained 
my understanding of how to learn languages,
how to follow that process.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.420
That's the beginning for me and it helped. Also uh...

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:44.080
It's just a wonderful hobby to have, right?

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:52.460
When you learn a niche language like that,
one that's only spoken in one country,
by not that many people, they appreciate it so much.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:56.840
When I learned Hebrew and I spoke to
Hebrew speakers, they couldn't believe it.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:03.120
They wondered why I would speak their language
out of all the languages of the world.
"why our language?" they had asked me.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.520
They say: "are you Jewish?"
and I'd say "No, I'm not Jewish."

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.420
And they'd be even more shocked,
which I was found kind of funny but...

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:17.580
That opened up a lot of... a lot of friendships 
and a lot of great interactions with people.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:24.480
And I got to know a lot of people really well
because they were just so impressed that
I loved their culture and love their language.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:31.440
So it's just a great experience to be all in 
a language that not that many people are studying.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.480
Because you really stand out to the people who speak it.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.740
Uh... another thing is...

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:44.780
A niche language can you ironically be
more marketable than a major language, right?

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:50.300
If, in your company, 50 people speak Spanish, 
like in Southern California,

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:55.060
I don't know how much of a marketable skill it is 
to study Spanish as a second language.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:58.480
Because so many other people speak it
and I'm from Vancouver.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.240
In Vancouver, if I studied Chinese, 
like Mandarin or Cantonese,

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:08.960
probably that's not really a marketable skill for me 
because so many other native speakers live there.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:11.220
And they're bilingual in english too. So...

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:15.900
They'll always have a leg up on me.
It's not really marketable but...

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:22.900
If I knew Burmese or I knew Thai...
Thai is not a minor language but, 
it's only spoken really in Thailand.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:31.000
If I know Thai, let's say, then that might be
more marketable in my company because
probably not that many other people speak Thai.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:37.000
So, if we're doing business with Thailand, I could be 
the person, the only person who can fill that position, right?

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:41.380
So don't assume that 
a niche language can't be marketable.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:45.160
You might have to look around 
and see how you can market it but...

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:47.520
it's not necessarily unmarketable.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:55.240
Some people learn a language for 
a slightly different kind of motivation.

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:59.040
They're really motivated by a goal or their dream.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:04.320
Not necessarily the language itself 
but the language helps them achieve their dream.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:10.340
Now here's what I mean. I have some acquaintances 
or some of my students here. I teach at a university.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:14.260
Some of my students are studying 
to be flight attendants.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:17.520
And they have to speak English to be a flight attendant.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:21.040
They don't really care about 
learning English itself necessarily.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:28.320
But they want to be flight attendants.
That's their dream job, they've sought after
since they were kids, always wanted that job.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:34.680
So, to help achieve their dream, they need English.
So the dream motivates them to learn the language.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:38.440
So, if you have a dream like that, 
if you have some goal for the future, then...

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:43.400
Maybe you should look at what language might fit in
with that goal, and then, that's the one you go with.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:49.180
Because your dream or goal will provide the
motivation and passion that will drive you,

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:52.580
and push you through 
all those speech bumps along the way.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.940
and give you that long term vision 
for studying the language.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.700
So, if you are, I don't know, ... 
if you're a filmmaker and you love French films,

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:07.460
and you really want to go to the Cannes Film Festival,
you want to schmooze or work with French filmmakers,

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.060
then maybe French is the language for you, right?

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:15.520
I have a friend who's a musician 
and she often travels to Austria.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:21.300
Because she says that the best instructors 
or the best piano mentors are in Austria.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:26.000
At least the ones she knows and 
I know Austria is well known for orchestras

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.580
and for music in general. So she's studying German.

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:34.260
Because that  helps her live out her dream 
or achieve her dream more fully,

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:38.020
by being in Austria, right? 
So that's the language for her.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.220
So whatever language helps you achieve your dream?
It might be the best one for you.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:45.740
So the basic point is: go with your passion.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:50.020
Unfortunately you don't choose your interests. 
Your interests choose you.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.720
So, if you were chosen by an interest in Chinese 
or in Spanish, then good for you.

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:00.820
If you were chosen by Esperanto, 
then 'sucks to be you but...

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:04.140
you're going to enjoy it because 
that's your passion. So go with it.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:07.200
And maybe in the future, 
you will choose a different language

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:14.160
and re-use a lot of what you learned and re-apply 
all of that new knowledge and experience.

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:16.560
Alright, so that's what I would say: 
go with your passion.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:22.280
And don't ignore that advice 
because you won't stick it out...

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:23.520
Alright, thanks!

